kkeeping; a first call that never finishes holds the route until its lease expires, then the next call retires the lease-only record as `lease expired unreleased` and mints under its own lease; a late first mint records `store moved on` and leaves the newer lease, or the finished generation-1 record, untouched; a raising first mint leaves neither record nor tombstone and frees the route; at dial zero, no store is written -> upward, these are exactly LLR-270's first-mint clauses (the lease-only record before launch, lease-only mints and only active/draining resumes, expiry retiring a crashed first call's record, the own-lease landing rule whether the taker runs or has finished, and keep_abandon's removal) and SR-227's \"let no two calls use one retained session at once\" for the route's first call. Sideways, it does not overlap TC-266 (the dial-0 inert shape), TC-267 (the general lease wait, release, and the contended-tombstone mechanism) or TC-268 (keep-warm); its dial-zero clause is the first-call specific of TC-266. Downward, each of the five named evidence tests exists in tests/test_session_keep.py and asserts what the Method states. The contended application of a lease-only tombstone shares `load_honoured` -> `_apply_tombstone` with the immediate path TC-329 drives, and TC-267 covers the deferral -> ready: every clause is an observable assertion with a named actor and outcome, nothing in LLR-270's first-mint text goes unverified, and nothing in the TC exceeds its parent.\n\nOUTCOME: APPROVE rows=1\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
